sections.body_md:
A **constructor** is a member method of a class that is executed automatically when an object is created with **new**; it is used to **initialise the data members**.

- It has the **same name as the class**.
- It has **no return type**, not even void.
- It is executed **once for each object**, by new. It cannot be called again through the object.

```java
class Parcel {
    int weight;
    double charge;
    Parcel() {
        weight = 500;
        charge = 35;
        System.out.println("Parcel created");
    }
    void show() {
        System.out.println(weight + " g, Rs " + charge);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parcel p = new Parcel();
        p.show();
    }
}
```

Output: `Parcel created / 500 g, Rs 35.0`.

Declaring a reference, `Parcel p;`, creates no object and runs no constructor: `Declared / Parcel created / Created` shows the order of a declaration, a creation and a print.

If no statement assigns a data member, it holds the **default value** of its type: 0 for int, 0.0 for double, false for boolean and null for a String or any object. The run of the class with four data members prints `0 / 0.0 / false / null`.

If a class has **no constructor**, the compiler supplies a **default constructor** (no parameters) so that `new Kiosk()` works. If the class has **any** constructor, the compiler supplies nothing.

```java
class Parcel {
    int weight;
    Parcel(int w) {
        weight = w;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parcel p = new Parcel();
    }
}
```

The compiler says: "constructor Parcel in class Parcel cannot be applied to given types;". To allow `new Parcel()` as well, write a no-argument constructor too.

A **parameterised constructor** has parameters; the starting values are passed as arguments in the new statement.

```java
class Rickshaw {
    int seats;
    double farePerKm;
    Rickshaw(int s, double f) {
        seats = s;
        farePerKm = f;
    }
    double fare(int km) {
        return farePerKm * km;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Rickshaw r = new Rickshaw(3, 12.5);
        System.out.println(r.seats + " " + r.fare(8));
    }
}
```

Output: `3 100.0`.

When a parameter has the name of a data member, the parameter hides it. The keyword **this** is the current object, so `this.name = name;` stores the parameter in the data member. Without this, `name = name;` leaves the data member at its default: `null 0` is displayed instead of `Kavya 15`.

A constructor may contain any statements, for example a decision that checks an argument: the Sack class gives `0 40` for the arguments -5 and 40.

**Constructor overloading** means writing several constructors in one class. They have the class name and differ in the **number**, **types** or **order** of their parameters; parameter names do not count (two constructors with one int each give "constructor Pack(int) is already defined in class Pack").

```java
class Meter {
    Meter() {
        System.out.println("no argument");
    }
    Meter(int n) {
        System.out.println("int " + n);
    }
    Meter(double d) {
        System.out.println("double " + d);
    }
    Meter(String s) {
        System.out.println("String " + s);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Meter();
        new Meter(5);
        new Meter(5.0);
        new Meter("5");
    }
}
```

Output: `no argument / int 5 / double 5.0 / String 5`.

The arguments of new decide which constructor runs.

**Exact match first.** If there is none, the argument is **widened**: char to int to long to float to double. With the four constructors of Meter, the char 'A' runs the int constructor (`int 65`), a long 7L runs the double one (`double 7.0`) and a float 2.5f runs the double one (`double 2.5`).

**Errors:** a call fails when no constructor accepts the arguments ("no suitable constructor found for Meter(boolean)") or when two fit equally well ("reference to Mix is ambiguous").

**Order:** each constructor runs when its own object is created, so the messages follow the order of the new statements:

```java
class Gate {
    int id;
    Gate() {
        id = 1;
        System.out.println("Default constructor");
    }
    Gate(int n) {
        id = n;
        System.out.println("Gate " + n + " built");
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Gate a = new Gate(7);
        Gate b = new Gate();
        Gate c = new Gate(3);
        System.out.println("a=" + a.id + " b=" + b.id + " c=" + c.id);
    }
}
```

Output: `Gate 7 built / Default constructor / Gate 3 built / a=7 b=1 c=3`.

A constructor may also take an object of its own class and copy its data members; the copy is a separate object (`12 20 99.5` for the Pack program of the slides).

