With **void** in front, the member is an ordinary method that only has the same name as the class; it is **not a constructor**. The new operator therefore does not call it, and the data members keep their default values.

```java
class Diya {
    int lit;
    void Diya() {
        lit = 5;
        System.out.println("Diya ready");
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Diya d = new Diya();
        System.out.println(d.lit);
    }
}
```

The program prints only `0`: the message of the method does not appear, and lit is 0.

Java gives each data member the default value of its type: **0** for int, **0.0** for double, **false** for boolean and **null** for String.

```java
